+
+#### Neural Network 3D Object Representation
+A method where a multilayer feed-forward neural network represents a 3D object by mapping 3D coordinates in object space to a binary output indicating membership [S6]. For objects with simple analytical forms (e.g., sphere, cube), specify the network's parameters manually [S6]. For complex shapes, generate training examples consisting of points on the object's surface, points on inclosed surfaces, and points on enclosing surfaces [S6]. Generate training data using a heuristic that uses the surface normal to determine if a point in the vicinity of the surface belongs to the inside or outside of the object [S6]. Train the network on the generated examples using the back propagation technique [S6]. Test the representation method by repeated evaluations of the network for points inside the object space [S6].
+
+#### Neural Graph Mapping
+A dynamic multi-field scene representation composed of small, lightweight neural fields anchored to keyframes in a pose graph [S7]. Fields deform with updated poses during loop closure, enabling consistent volumetric mapping without costly reintegration or fixed scene boundaries [S7]. Merges accurate sparse visual tracking with dense neural mapping in an RGB-D SLAM framework [S7].
+
+#### NeuRBF
+A hybrid neural field model that enhances representation accuracy and compactness by combining adaptive radial basis functions (RBFs) with grid-based RBF interpolation [S7]. Introduce multi-frequency sinusoidal composition to extend the frequencies encoded by each basis function [S7]. Decode these features by an MLP for volume rendering with SDF reconstruction capabilities [S7].
 
 ## Background
-Neural representations for volume rendering emerged from the need to compress high-dimensional scene data and enable novel view synthesis from sparse inputs [G]. The foundational work on Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) demonstrated that continuous volumetric scenes could be represented by MLPs [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent developments introduced hash grids to accelerate training and inference [S1, unofficial][S3], and primitive-based methods to enable real-time rendering [S1, unofficial][S3]. The family has evolved to include hybrid approaches that combine the geometric precision of implicit fields with the rendering efficiency of explicit primitives, such as Neural Anchors [S2] and Structure-aware primitive learning [S2]. These methods are widely used in computer graphics for 3D reconstruction, virtual reality, and medical imaging [S1, unofficial][S2, S3].
+Neural implicit representations have seen growing interest in representing geometric information in a parametrized manner in neural networks [S1, unofficial]. Early works by Liu et al. [41] proposed the first usage of neural implicit representations in differentiable rendering, selecting the 3D point with maximum occupancy probability along a ray [S1, unofficial]. Mildenhall et al. [42] proposed differentiable rendering for neural transparency, computing pixel values using volume rendering by weighing values of all sampled points along a ray, supporting RGB images and view-dependent phenomena [S1, unofficial]. Level-set methods [43-46] represent surfaces as the zero level-set of a function, typically distance to the boundary [S1, unofficial].
 
